(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 29-year-old dental hygienist develops a painful, swollen index finger with grouped vesicles on an erythematous base, preceded by burning and tingling. A colleague suggests incision and drainage. What is the correct management?","choices":["Observe without treatment or precautions, since the lesion is self-limited","Do not incise and drain; cover the lesions, use hand hygiene, and consider early oral antiviral therapy","Incise and drain, then start oral antivirals","Apply a topical antifungal and keep the hand dry","Incise and drain the lesion, then start oral antibiotics for presumed bacterial paronychia of the finger"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Incision and drainage does not treat herpes simplex virus and can delay healing. Lesions should be covered, hand hygiene used, contact with mucosa and broken skin avoided until healed, and early oral acyclovir, valacyclovir, or famciclovir may shorten the episode. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has an itchy, sharply demarcated plaque on the proximal medial thigh extending from the crural fold. The scrotum is not involved. He also has scaling between his toes. What does the scrotal sparing indicate?","choices":["It has no diagnostic significance","It indicates erythrasma rather than a dermatophyte infection","It argues against tinea cruris and toward a process in which dermatophytes are not involved","It indicates inverse psoriasis","It supports tinea cruris, since scrotal involvement would suggest candidal intertrigo instead"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. In tinea cruris the scrotum is typically spared, and scrotal involvement suggests candidal intertrigo, which commonly involves the scrotum. His coexisting tinea pedis fits, since the two often occur together. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 7-month-old girl has a diaper rash confined to the convex surfaces of the buttocks and upper thighs, with the inguinal folds clearly spared. There are no satellite lesions, no bullae, and no purulent drainage. Her mother reports frequent loose stools over the past week. What is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["A high-potency topical corticosteroid applied under the diaper twice daily until the rash has cleared","Leaving the diaper off entirely until the rash resolves","A topical antifungal applied at every diaper change","Oral cephalexin","Frequent diaper changes with gentle cleansing and a thick zinc oxide or petrolatum barrier at every change"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The foundation of treatment is reducing exposure to moisture and irritants through frequent changes, gentle cleansing, air exposure, and superabsorbent diapers, with a thick barrier layer applied at each change. The previous layer need not be scrubbed away if it remains clean. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man is about to be started on carbamazepine for newly diagnosed epilepsy. Which human leukocyte antigen association should prompt caution before prescribing it?","choices":["Human leukocyte antigen B*15:02","Human leukocyte antigen B*58:01","Human leukocyte antigen DQ2","Human leukocyte antigen B27","Human leukocyte antigen Cw6"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Human leukocyte antigen B*15:02 is the risk allele associated with carbamazepine, while human leukocyte antigen B*58:01 is associated with allopurinol. Both are listed risk factors for Stevens-Johnson syndrome. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man is diagnosed with dermatitis herpetiformis and dapsone is planned for rapid symptom control. What must be checked before the drug is started, and what must be monitored afterwards?","choices":["Check thyroid function beforehand, and monitor the complete blood count for haemolytic anaemia","Check glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status beforehand, and monitor renal function for nephrotoxicity, proteinuria, and rising creatinine","Check liver function beforehand, and monitor for photosensitivity","No pre-treatment testing is required, and no specific monitoring is needed","Check glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status beforehand, and monitor the complete blood count for haemolytic anaemia and methaemoglobinaemia"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency must be checked before initiating dapsone, and the complete blood count is monitored for haemolytic anaemia and methaemoglobinaemia. Dapsone at 25 to 200 mg daily gives relief within days. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with recurrent genital herpes simplex virus type 2 infection is in a relationship with an uninfected partner. Which treatment approach also reduces transmission risk?","choices":["No antiviral therapy, since transmission occurs only during visible outbreaks","Topical acyclovir applied to lesions during outbreaks","Patient-initiated episodic therapy at the first sign of a recurrence","Daily suppressive valacyclovir","Treatment of the uninfected partner with prophylactic valacyclovir"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent genital herpes may be managed with patient-initiated episodic therapy or daily suppressive therapy, and suppressive valacyclovir lowers herpes simplex virus type 2 transmission. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man from Connecticut presents with a swollen, painful knee two years after an untreated tick bite. What is the classic manifestation of stage 3 late persistent Lyme disease?","choices":["Symmetric polyarthritis of the small joints of the hands","Erythema migrans at the site of the tick bite","Cranial nerve palsies and meningitis","A rash beginning on the ankles and wrists and spreading centrally toward the trunk over days","Monoarticular or oligoarticular arthritis affecting the knee or other weight-bearing joints"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Stage 3 occurs months to years later, affecting skin, central nervous system, and musculoskeletal systems, with monoarticular or oligoarticular arthritis of the knee or weight-bearing joints as the classic manifestation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man presents with a two-month history of intense itching followed by blistering. Examination shows numerous 1 to 3 cm tense, firm bullae on the trunk, flexural surfaces of the arms, and the axillary and inguinal folds. Rubbing the skin adjacent to a blister does not cause the epidermis to slough. There is a single small erosion in the mouth. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dermatitis herpetiformis","Bullous pemphigoid","Erythema multiforme","Pemphigus vulgaris","Bullous impetigo"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bullous pemphigoid damages the epithelial basement membrane, producing subepithelial blistering, and classically presents after age 60 with tense 1 to 3 cm bullae on the trunk, extremity flexures, and axillary and inguinal folds. A pruritic prodrome may precede the bullae by weeks to months, and Nikolsky's sign is negative. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man presents with an itchy, scaling annular plaque on the trunk. The clinician wishes to confirm the presence of fungal elements at the bedside before starting treatment. What is the most appropriate test?","choices":["Tzanck smear","Potassium hydroxide wet preparation","Mineral oil preparation","Nail clipping with periodic acid-Schiff stain","Patch testing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A potassium hydroxide wet preparation detects fungal elements in skin, hair, or nail samples, and it is the appropriate bedside confirmation before treating a suspected dermatophyte infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man presents with an engorged tick attached to his flank after a hike in an endemic area. What single action is emphasised first in the management of a tick-borne illness such as Lyme disease?","choices":["Administer prophylactic intravenous antibiotics to all patients","Send the tick for species identification before any treatment","Remove the tick immediately","Apply a topical corticosteroid to the bite site","Await the appearance of erythema migrans before acting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The management sequence begins with removing the tick immediately, followed by antibiotics, which are indicated in all stages. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman has a sacral wound showing full-thickness loss of skin extending into the dermis, and a second area on the heel showing partial loss involving only the epidermis. What are these two findings called?","choices":["Fissure and excoriation, respectively","Ulceration and erosion, respectively","Scar and scale, respectively","Excoriation and lichenification, respectively","Erosion and ulceration, respectively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ulceration is full-thickness loss of the skin extending to the dermis or deeper, whereas erosion is partial loss of the skin of only the epidermis. Depth is the entire distinction. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with cellulitis of the leg is warned that the area may look worse on the first day of antibiotics. What explains this, and when should therapy be reconsidered?","choices":["This indicates devitalised tissue requiring immediate debridement","The antibiotic is failing and should be changed immediately on day one","The patient has developed an allergy to the antibiotic","Sudden destruction of pathogens releases potent enzymes that increase local inflammation; therapy should be reconsidered only after seven full days","Sudden destruction of pathogens releases enzymes that increase local inflammation; therapy should be reconsidered if fever persists beyond 48 hours"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Cellulitis may look and feel worse during the first day because sudden destruction of pathogens releases potent enzymes that increase local inflammation. Fever usually resolves within 24 hours, and persistence beyond 48 hours should prompt a change in antimicrobial therapy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 12-year-old girl presents with fever and headache four days after a tick bite, and a rash has now appeared. What is the clinical triad of Rocky Mountain spotted fever, and how often is it present?","choices":["Fever, arthritis, and erythema migrans, present in most patients with the infection at presentation","Fever, cough, and rash, present in about 60 percent","Fever, headache, and rash, present in essentially all patients","Rash, pruritus, and lymphadenopathy","Fever above 39.5 degrees Celsius, headache, and rash, present in only about 60 percent of patients"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The clinical triad is fever above 39.5 degrees Celsius, headache, and rash, present in only about 60 percent of patients. Fever presents in the first 3 days with rash following 2 to 4 days after fever onset. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has confirmed tungiasis of both feet after a trip abroad. What management is appropriate?","choices":["Professional extermination of the home","Surgical excision or cryotherapy with topical agents, tetanus prophylaxis, and systemic antibiotics, with prevention by not walking barefoot","Oral albendazole for 3 days","Symptomatic treatment alone with antihistamines, oatmeal baths, and antipruritic lotions, without removing the embedded flea from the skin","Topical permethrin applied overnight to the whole body"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Management includes surgical excision or cryotherapy and topical agents, tetanus prophylaxis, and systemic antibiotics. Prevention centres on avoiding walking barefoot in endemic areas. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman has a firm nodule on the lower leg and reports that it occasionally hurts when knocked. What is notable about the symptom profile of a dermatofibroma?","choices":["Symptoms occur only in lesions larger than 3 cm","It is invariably painful and never asymptomatic","It is always pruritic and never painful","It is usually asymptomatic but is the most common painful skin tumour when it does cause symptoms","It becomes painful only after malignant transformation has occurred within the lesion itself"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dermatofibroma is usually asymptomatic, sometimes with a history of insect bite, and when symptomatic may cause slight pruritus or pain — it is described as the most common painful skin tumour. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"The mother of a 3-year-old boy with atopic dermatitis asks how to apply the emollient she has been given. What instruction is correct?","choices":["Apply the emollient only to areas that are actively inflamed","Withhold the emollient on days a topical corticosteroid is used","Bathe the child frequently in hot water before applying the emollient","Apply the emollient to the skin after rinsing, and avoid irritants and fragranced skin care","Apply a fragranced moisturiser to improve the child's willingness to be treated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prevention advice is that emollient should be applied to the skin after rinsing, the patient should avoid irritants, and skin care should be fragrance-free. Clinicians should also demonstrate medication quantity and correct application. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man has a crusted genital lesion that has been present for six days. What is the preferred diagnostic test for a suspected herpes simplex virus lesion, and how should a negative result on an older lesion be interpreted?","choices":["A potassium hydroxide preparation from the ulcer base","A Tzanck smear from a fresh vesicle, which distinguishes herpes simplex virus from varicella-zoster virus and gives a definitive result without confirmation by polymerase chain reaction","A type-specific nucleic acid amplification test from a fresh vesicle, ulcer base, or crust; a negative result on an older lesion does not exclude infection because shedding is intermittent","Viral culture, with a negative result reliably excluding infection","Herpes simplex virus immunoglobulin M serology"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Swabbing a fresh vesicle, ulcer base, or crust for type-specific nucleic acid amplification testing is the preferred test. A negative swab from an older lesion does not exclude infection because shedding is intermittent, and culture is less sensitive in healing or recurrent lesions. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman has a firm, bulbous nodule at the site of an ear piercing performed two years ago. The lesion extends well beyond the original piercing site and has continued to enlarge. What distinguishes this from a hypertrophic scar?","choices":["It remains confined within the original wound margins and flattens over time","It extends beyond the original wound margins and shows no regression with time","It developed rapidly within four weeks of the injury","It is always painless","It regresses and flattens with time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A keloid extends beyond the wound site margins, develops slowly, continues to enlarge for months to years, and shows no regression with a tendency to recur. A hypertrophic scar develops rapidly within four weeks, remains within the wound margins, and flattens with time. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man presents with an abrupt eruption of small pustules on the thighs and buttocks. Each pustule sits on an erythematous base and is pierced by a central hair. He is afebrile with no systemic symptoms. He has type 2 diabetes and wears occlusive workout clothing. What is the most likely causative organism?","choices":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Streptococcus pyogenes","Candida albicans","Corynebacterium minutissimum","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Bacterial folliculitis is most commonly caused by Staphylococcus aureus. His diabetes and occlusive clothing are both listed risk factors, alongside obesity, poor hygiene, heat and humidity, immunocompromise, corticosteroids, and nasal carriage. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 58-year-old Black woman has had a pigmented band in the great toenail for two years that was previously attributed to trauma. Why is diagnostic delay a recurring theme in nail unit melanoma, and where does the tumour usually arise?","choices":["It occurs only in fair-skinned patients with heavy cumulative sun exposure, and it arises most often in the nail bed","It always presents with pain, which patients ignore","It arises in the periungual skin and is easily seen","It is rare and not clearly ultraviolet driven so may occur in any skin tone, and it arises most often in the matrix","It is common and therefore over-diagnosed rather than delayed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nail unit melanoma is a rare acral melanoma arising most often in the matrix. It is not clearly ultraviolet driven and may occur in any skin tone, and delayed recognition contributes to worse outcomes. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman presents with painful genital ulcers. The clinician considers alternative causes. Which differential is characteristically painless?","choices":["Chancroid, caused by Haemophilus ducreyi, producing painful necrotizing ulcers with inguinal lymphadenopathy","Candidiasis","Syphilis, whose lesions start as solitary raised papules that erode and are usually painless","Trauma","Herpes simplex virus recurrence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Syphilis lesions start as solitary raised papules that erode and are usually painless, which is what distinguishes them from the painful ulcers of herpes simplex virus and chancroid. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with vitiligo affecting more than 5 percent of her body surface area is being counselled on treatment. What is first-line, and why is one phototherapy option preferred over another?","choices":["Topical therapy alone is first line at any extent of disease","Psoralen with ultraviolet A is preferred over narrow band ultraviolet B because it carries fewer adverse effects and no increase in skin cancer risk at any body surface area involved","Phototherapy is first line above 5 percent body surface area, with narrow band ultraviolet B preferred over psoralen with ultraviolet A because the latter increases skin cancer risk","No treatment is available, so counselling alone is appropriate","Surgical grafting is first line for all patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Phototherapy is first line in patients with more than 5 percent body surface area involvement, and narrow band ultraviolet B is preferred over psoralen with ultraviolet A, which carries adverse effects including increased skin cancer risk. Combination of topical therapy with phototherapy is ideal. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man with progressive frontotemporal recession and early vertex thinning asks about treatment. He and his partner are not planning a pregnancy and he has no contraindications. Which statement about the two principal medical therapies is correct?","choices":["The sexual adverse effects reported with finasteride are permanent once they occur","Neither agent alters the course of the condition, so no medical therapy should be offered to this patient","Finasteride is a topical agent and minoxidil is the oral component of combination therapy","Finasteride is an oral 5-alpha-reductase type 2 inhibitor and works best in combination with topical minoxidil","Minoxidil is primarily effective at the frontal hairline rather than the crown"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Finasteride prevents further hair loss and may provide some regrowth, and works best in combination with minoxidil. Dihydrotestosterone is the androgen chiefly responsible for the follicular miniaturisation, which is what the enzyme inhibition addresses. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a rapidly expanding painful ulcer with undermined violaceous borders, and repeated wound cultures are negative. What is the most accurate description of how pyoderma gangrenosum is diagnosed?","choices":["It is confirmed by wound culture growing a characteristic organism","It is confirmed by a specific biopsy finding that establishes the diagnosis definitively on its own","It is confirmed by the presence of inflammatory bowel disease","It is a diagnosis of exclusion, requiring infectious, vascular, and other causes to be ruled out","It is confirmed by a positive pathergy skin test in all patients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pyoderma gangrenosum is explicitly a diagnosis of exclusion. Infectious ulcers, which are culture negative in pyoderma gangrenosum, and vascular insufficiency ulcers assessed by ankle-brachial index must be ruled out. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 6-year-old unvaccinated child has a generalised pruritic eruption concentrated on the trunk, scalp, and face. Macules, papules, vesicles, and crusts are all present at the same time. What feature is most characteristic?","choices":["Lesions limited to the palms and soles","Lesions with honey-coloured crusting around the nose and mouth","Lesions in multiple stages of healing appearing simultaneously","Lesions confined to a single dermatome","Lesions all at the same stage of evolution"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Primary varicella-zoster virus infection causes a generalised pruritic eruption in multiple stages of healing — macules progress to papules, vesicles, and crusts, with several stages appearing simultaneously. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man is diagnosed with pityriasis rosea. The eruption began four days ago and is mildly itchy. He is otherwise well and not pregnant-exposed or immunocompromised. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Oral terbinafine","Oral acyclovir for all patients","Reassurance alone that the condition is self-limited, with no treatment offered for the pruritus he has reported","Reassurance that the condition is self-limited, with an oral antihistamine or topical steroid used cautiously for itch","Oral prednisone tapered over two weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pityriasis rosea is self-limited and the mainstay is reassurance. Oral antihistamines or topical steroids can be used cautiously for pruritus, and phototherapy or natural sunlight may help if begun in the first week of the eruption. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 171"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman is told she has multiple actinic keratoses and asks whether that means she has skin cancer. What is the relationship between actinic keratosis and keratinocyte carcinoma?","choices":["Actinic keratosis progresses to basal cell carcinoma","Actinic keratosis is entirely benign and unrelated to carcinoma","Actinic keratosis is a form of invasive keratinocyte carcinoma from the outset rather than a precursor lesion","Actinic keratosis progresses to melanoma","Actinic keratosis is premalignant and lies on a biologic continuum with keratinocyte carcinoma"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Chronic ultraviolet injury produces dysplastic keratinocytic change in a field of sun-damaged skin. Actinic keratosis is premalignant and lies on a biologic continuum with keratinocyte carcinoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has had hidradenitis suppurativa of both axillae for eight years despite antibiotics and repeated drainage, and asks whether anything can cure it. What offers the best chance of permanent cure?","choices":["Wide excision of the affected areas","Long-term systemic antibiotics","Topical clindamycin used indefinitely","Intralesional triamcinolone acetonide injections","Incision and drainage of each cyst as it develops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chances of permanent cure are best with wide excision of the affected areas. Large fluctuant cysts are incised and drained, but that manages an episode rather than curing the disease. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has not been taking a medication prescribed for her three months ago and is reluctant to say why. Which barriers to treatment and medication adherence should be considered?","choices":["Only the patient's motivation","Only the cost of medications","Cost and affordability, low health literacy or lack of understanding, and cultural or religious factors","Barriers to adherence are unpredictable, cannot be anticipated in advance, and are best addressed only after a treatment failure","Only the complexity of the dosing regimen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Barriers to treatment and medication adherence include the cost and affordability of medications or services, low health literacy or lack of understanding, and cultural or religious considerations. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman presents with painful grouped vesicles on an erythematous base. The clinician performs a bedside Tzanck smear, which shows cytologic changes consistent with a herpesvirus. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Obtain a bacterial culture and sensitivity","Perform direct immunofluorescence on perilesional skin","Accept the Tzanck smear as definitive and identify the specific virus from it","Perform a potassium hydroxide preparation","Confirm with viral polymerase chain reaction"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A Tzanck smear provides rapid evaluation of vesicular lesions for herpesvirus cytologic changes, but polymerase chain reaction is preferred for confirmation. Viral polymerase chain reaction detects the genetic material of the virus and identifies which herpesvirus is responsible. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy and his mother, who teaches at his school, both present with honey-crusted perioral lesions. In which patient with impetigo should a culture be obtained rather than relying on clinical appearance alone?","choices":["Any patient being treated with topical mupirocin","Any child under five years of age","Only patients who have already failed a course of oral antibiotics for the same skin infection","A healthcare worker or teacher, who is at high risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus","Any patient with honey-coloured crusting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cultures are obtained if the patient is at high risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus infection, with healthcare workers and teachers given as the examples, or if acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is a concern. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 9-year-old presents with a fever above 39.5 degrees Celsius for three days, headache, and now a rash that began on the ankles and wrists and is spreading centrally. What is the treatment, and does his age change it?","choices":["Supportive care only, since the illness is self-limited","Withhold antibiotics until the indirect immunofluorescence assay returns, since it is the gold standard test for this infection","Amoxicillin, because doxycycline is contraindicated under the age of 8","Intravenous ceftriaxone for 14 days","Doxycycline 2.2 mg per kilogram by mouth every 12 hours for 5 to 10 days; doxycycline is used in children for this infection"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Rocky Mountain spotted fever is treated with doxycycline in adults at 100 mg every 12 hours for 5 to 10 days, the same in pregnant women, and in children at 2.2 mg per kilogram every 12 hours. The illness is life threatening if untreated. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with papulopustular rosacea has Demodex-associated disease that has not responded to topical metronidazole. What topical agent is described as superior for this situation?","choices":["A high-potency topical corticosteroid","Metronidazole 0.75 to 1 percent gel at a higher frequency","Azelaic acid 15 to 20 percent","Brimonidine gel","Ivermectin 1 percent cream"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Ivermectin 1 percent cream is described as superior to metronidazole for Demodex-associated papulopustular rosacea, reflecting the contribution of Demodex folliculorum mite overgrowth to the pathogenesis. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 2-week-old neonate develops blisters at sites of handling and diapering, and genetic testing is planned. Which subtype carries the greatest long-term risk of squamous cell carcinoma and requires the most intensive multidisciplinary surveillance?","choices":["Kindler syndrome","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex","Acquired epidermolysis bullosa","Junctional epidermolysis bullosa","Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa causes severe scarring, and the multidisciplinary team includes haematology and oncology specifically for squamous cell carcinoma surveillance. Scarring subtypes carry the malignancy risk. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with diabetes has an asymptomatic brownish patch in a skin fold noted on routine examination. Where is erythrasma most commonly found, and what is the typical symptom profile?","choices":["The inner thighs, crural region, scrotum, and between the fourth and fifth toes, usually asymptomatic and sometimes pruritic","Around the nose and mouth, with honey-coloured crusting","The scalp, thighs, trunk, axilla, and inguinal region, with follicular pustules","The axilla, groin, breasts, and perineum, with recurrent painful suppurative lesions occurring more than twice in six months","The face and lower extremity, with sudden onset of high fever"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The most common sites are the inner thighs, crural region, scrotum, and between the fourth and fifth toes, with the axilla, under the breasts, and intergluteal folds less common. It is usually asymptomatic and may be pruritic. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has confirmed toxic epidermal necrolysis with epidermal detachment of 45 percent of his body surface area. What is the mandatory disposition?","choices":["Outpatient management with daily wound review","Observation in the emergency department until the eruption stabilises","Admission to a general medical ward with dermatology consultation","Admission to a burn unit or intensive care unit","Admission to a surgical ward for debridement of detached epidermis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Burn unit or intensive care admission is mandatory for all cases of toxic epidermal necrolysis, together with immediate discontinuation of all suspect drugs, fluid resuscitation estimated as for major burns, and non-adhesive biological dressings. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl has multiple 1 to 2 mm firm skin-coloured papules symmetrically distributed on the lower eyelids that appeared around puberty. What is the origin of these lesions?","choices":["Benign neoplasms of apocrine glands","Proliferation of dermal capillaries","Benign neoplasms of eccrine ducts","Collections of lipid-laden macrophages","Hyperplasia of sebaceous glands around a central pore"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Syringomas are benign neoplasms of eccrine sweat ducts. They appear at puberty and are more common in females, presenting as multiple 1 to 2 mm papules and usually asymptomatic. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A clinician has settled quickly on a diagnosis for a 50-year-old man and is now gathering data that would support it. Which suggestion for good decision-making addresses confirmation bias most directly?","choices":["Defer all decisions until every test result has returned","Apply an if-then guideline rule as early as possible","Ask questions to disprove, rather than confirm, your working diagnosis","Rely on intuitive pattern recognition to reach a conclusion quickly","Gather as much data as possible regardless of relevance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Among the suggestions for good decision-making is to ask questions to disprove rather than confirm, alongside slowing down, being aware of base rates, considering what data is truly relevant, and actively seeking alternative diagnoses. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman of Mediterranean descent presents with painful mouth erosions that began three months ago, followed by skin lesions. Examination shows flaccid bullae and widespread crusted erosions on the scalp, chest, axillae, and groin that bleed easily and are painful. Lateral pressure on normal-appearing skin causes the superficial layers to slip away. What does the biopsy most likely demonstrate?","choices":["Band-like lymphocytic infiltration of the upper dermis","Subepithelial blistering without acantholysis","Acantholysis, with intraepithelial blister formation","Neutrophils aligned in a straight narrow row at the dermal-epidermal junction","Granular immunoglobulin A deposits at the tips of the dermal papillae"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pemphigus is characterised by loss of keratinocyte-to-keratinocyte adhesion, termed acantholysis, producing intraepithelial blisters. The oral onset, flaccid easily ruptured bullae, and positive Nikolsky's sign all identify pemphigus, and biopsy demonstrating acantholysis is the diagnostic finding. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 132"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with tinea corporis asks whether the infection can spread to her mouth, and a student asks why dermatophytes never involve mucous membranes. What is the correct explanation?","choices":["Dermatophytes require sebum, which mucous membranes do not produce","Dermatophytes are killed by salivary enzymes but survive elsewhere","Dermatophytes can involve mucous membranes when the patient is immunocompromised","Dermatophytes are obligate intracellular organisms confined to the nuclei of living keratinocytes","Dermatophytes infect and survive only on dead keratin, so they cannot survive on mucous membranes"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Dermatophytes infect and survive only on dead keratin, involving the superficial portion of the skin at the stratum corneum, plus hair and nails. They cannot survive on mucous membranes. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 32-year-old fair-skinned woman has numerous moles under 6 mm with homogeneous surface and colour. One lesion is very dark brown to black. What is the significance of that lesion?","choices":["A very dark brown or black naevus on a light-skinned individual is suspicious and warrants further evaluation","It confirms melanoma without need for biopsy","A very dark naevus is expected in patients with numerous moles and requires no further evaluation or biopsy","It indicates a Spitz nevus, which is always benign","It represents a blue nevus and requires no evaluation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Common acquired melanocytic naevi are usually less than 6 mm with homogeneous surface and colour, round to oval with sharp demarcation. A very dark brown or black lesion on a light-skinned individual is specifically flagged as suspicious. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man presents with sudden pain and swelling over the sacrum in the gluteal cleft. Examination shows a warm, tender, erythematous fluctuant mass with purulent drainage. What is the appropriate management of this acute presentation?","choices":["Magnetic resonance imaging before any intervention","Incision and drainage","Warm compresses and observation","Immediate wide surgical excision of the natal cleft","Oral antibiotics alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An acute pilonidal abscess requires incision and drainage. The condition arises when a dimple over the coccyx draws in hair and debris leading to follicular plugging, and ingrown hairs prevent drainage and promote abscess formation. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has a biopsy-proven keratoacanthoma on the dorsal forearm. What is the standard of care, and what margin is used for elliptical excision?","choices":["Observation alone, since the lesion regresses spontaneously within months of onset","Excision or destruction of the tumour, with elliptical excision using 5 mm margins","Excision with 1 mm margins","Intralesional corticosteroid injection","Cryotherapy without histological assessment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The standard of care is to excise or destroy the tumour, preferred because of possible malignancy, with elliptical excision using 5 mm margins. Mohs surgery may be indicated for large or recurrent lesions or those in cosmetically sensitive locations. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man has a biopsy-proven invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma on the dorsal forearm. What is the preferred management, and what follow-up is recommended?","choices":["Topical imiquimod or fluorouracil, with annual examination","Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery, with at least annual skin and lymph node examination","Radiotherapy as first-line treatment for all patients","Cryotherapy, with no routine follow-up","Observation with no scheduled follow-up, since cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma does not metastasise or recur"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery is preferred for invasive squamous cell carcinoma, with at least annual skin and lymph node examination and closer intervals for high-risk or immunosuppressed patients. All suspected invasive lesions are referred to dermatology or an experienced surgeon. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Computed tomography of a 48-year-old man with suspected necrotizing fasciitis shows gas in the soft tissue fascial planes. What does this indicate about the likely organism?","choices":["Gas may be present with Clostridium perfringens infection and is not present with group A streptococcal infection","Gas is present in all cases of necrotizing fasciitis regardless of organism","Gas is present with group A streptococcal infection and is absent with Clostridium perfringens infection on imaging","Gas indicates the infection is superficial rather than involving deep fascia","Gas indicates a fungal rather than bacterial cause"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gas may be present with Clostridium perfringens infection but is not present with group A streptococcal infection. Ultrasound is also useful for demonstrating air bubbles in soft tissue. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 129"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman has a blue-grey papule under 1 cm on the dorsum of the hand. What are blue naevi composed of, and how does management differ by lesion size?","choices":["Proliferating keratinocytes, requiring cryotherapy","Deeply pigmented spindle or epithelioid melanocytes in the dermis, diagnosed clinically when small and by biopsy when larger","Deeply pigmented spindle or epithelioid melanocytes in the epidermis, always diagnosed clinically whatever the size of the lesion","Lipid-laden macrophages, requiring lipid screening","Dilated dermal capillaries, blanching with pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blue naevi are composed of deeply pigmented spindle or epithelioid melanocytes in the dermis. Diagnosis is clinical for small lesions and by biopsy for larger ones, with observation and biopsy or excision if changes are noted. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman is to begin oral isotretinoin for recalcitrant nodular acne. What safety requirements apply?","choices":["Monthly liver function tests, with no pregnancy testing required","Negative pregnancy tests are mandatory before starting, monthly during treatment, and five weeks after completion, with only one month dispensed at a time","No specific requirements, since isotretinoin is used as monotherapy","A negative pregnancy test before starting and one week after completion","A negative pregnancy test before starting only, with no monthly testing during treatment and no further testing after the course is completed at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Isotretinoin is a potent teratogen. Negative pregnancy tests are mandatory prior to initiation, monthly, and 5 weeks post treatment, only one month is dispensed at a time, and providers must be registered. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy has eight light brown macules greater than 5 mm and freckling in both axillae. What condition does this suggest, and what is the freckling called?","choices":["Neurofibromatosis type 1, with intertriginous freckling known as Darier sign","Neurofibromatosis type 2, with freckling from the NF2 gene on chromosome 22","Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation from chronic friction","Multiple lentigines, with freckling caused by sun exposure","Neurofibromatosis type 1, with intertriginous freckling known as Crowe's sign"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Café au lait spots are often the first manifestation of neurofibromatosis type 1, defined as light tan to brown macules over 5 mm prepubertally. Intertriginous freckling in the axillary and inguinal regions is Crowe's sign, with freckles smaller than 5 mm. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has grouped papules and papulopustules around the mouth, and the clinician is distinguishing perioral dermatitis from its principal differential diagnoses. What finding would most specifically point to acne vulgaris instead?","choices":["The presence of open or closed comedones","Central facial erythema with flushing and telangiectasia","The presence of papulopustules on the face","Sparing of a narrow zone adjacent to the vermilion border","Prominent scale involving the scalp, eyebrows, and nasolabial folds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acne vulgaris is more likely when open or closed comedones are present. Perioral dermatitis produces grouped monomorphic papules, papulovesicles, or papulopustules without comedones. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has a painful vesicular eruption in a band across one side of the chest. What is the single most characteristic distributional feature of herpes zoster?","choices":["It follows the lines of skin cleavage over the trunk in a Christmas tree pattern following a solitary herald patch","It crosses the midline symmetrically in a bilateral band","It is generalised over the trunk, scalp, and face","It is confined to one or two adjacent sensory nerve distributions and stops abruptly at the midline without crossing it","It is confined to intertriginous folds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Blisters are confined to the cutaneous distribution of one or two adjacent sensory nerves and abruptly stop at the midline. Thoracic dermatomes are most frequently involved at about 55 percent, followed by cranial at about 20 percent. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has soft yellow plaques on the medial aspects of both upper eyelids. What do they contain, and what should be done?","choices":["Lipid-laden macrophages, and he should be screened for hyperlipidaemia","Eccrine duct neoplasms appearing at puberty","Lipid-laden macrophages, requiring no further investigation since the lesions are cosmetic","Keratin within an enclosed epithelial cyst, requiring excision","Dilated capillaries requiring laser therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Xanthelasma consists of soft yellow cholesterol plaques formed by collections of lipid-laden macrophages. They are associated with lipid disorders, so screening for hyperlipidaemia is indicated, and they may signify increased cardiac risk. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has recurrent grouped bites appearing each morning after sleeping in her own bed. What is essential in the management of a bedbug infestation beyond treating the patient's skin?","choices":["A professional exterminator is necessary to eradicate the infestation","Washing all bedding at 60 degrees Celsius is sufficient without any other measure","Applying a pediculicide to the affected skin","No environmental action is needed since the infestation is self-limited","Treating all household contacts with topical permethrin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management of the patient is symptomatic with local wound care, topical antiseptic or antibiotic cream for secondary infection, and topical corticosteroids or oral antihistamines for pruritus. A professional exterminator is necessary to eradicate the infestation itself. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man is diagnosed with a melanoma on the upper back that had spread radially for months before entering a vertical growth phase. What is the most common subtype of melanoma, and what proportion of cases does it represent?","choices":["Superficial spreading melanoma, about two-thirds of cases, arising on intermittently sun-exposed skin","Nodular melanoma, about two-thirds of cases","Lentigo maligna melanoma, about two-thirds of cases, arising on the chronically sun-exposed skin of older adults","Acral lentiginous melanoma, about two-thirds of cases","Amelanotic melanoma, about half of cases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Superficial spreading melanoma accounts for about two-thirds of cases, arises on intermittently sun-exposed skin, and evolves radially before entering vertical growth. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A student uses the VINDICATE mnemonic to build a differential for a 72-year-old man brought to the emergency department with confusion. What do the letters stand for?","choices":["Vascular, infectious, neoplastic, degenerative, iatrogenic, congenital, autoimmune, trauma, and endocrine or metabolic","Vascular, inflammatory, neurological, drug-related, idiopathic, cardiac, allergic, toxic, and environmental","A sequence of steps for physical examination","A checklist for evaluating the quality of published evidence","A scoring system for estimating pretest probability"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. VINDICATE is a category-based framework: vascular, infectious, neoplastic, degenerative, iatrogenic, congenital, autoimmune, trauma, and endocrine or metabolic. Applied to confusion it yields stroke, meningitis, brain tumour, Alzheimer disease, and so on. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 43-year-old factory worker splashes a concentrated alkali solution on the forearm, while a colleague of the same age has had weeks of soreness from repeated soap exposure. How does the timing of an irritant reaction to a strong alkali differ from that of a reaction to a mild irritant such as soap?","choices":["Alkalis produce symptoms over weeks while soaps produce symptoms within minutes","Both alkalis and soaps produce symptoms only after several weeks of repeated exposure","Alkalis and acids may produce symptoms within minutes or delayed up to 24 hours or more, whereas soaps produce subacute symptoms progressing over weeks","Neither alkalis nor soaps produce symptoms until a delayed type IV hypersensitivity reaction develops after 48 to 72 hours of exposure to the agent","Alkalis produce a systemic reaction while soaps produce a local one"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms present differently depending on the exposure: mild irritants such as soaps may present with subacute symptoms that progress over weeks, while alkalis and acids may present minutes after exposure or delayed up to 24 hours or more. Concentrated agents can lead to chemical burns and necrosis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a small area of cellulitis of the forearm with minimal pain, no systemic signs, and no comorbidity. In which patient with cellulitis is no diagnostic workup required?","choices":["A patient with a limited area of involvement, minimal pain, no systemic signs, and no risk factor for serious illness","Any patient whose cellulitis involves the lower leg, which is the most common site of involvement in adults","Only patients under 40 years of age","Any patient who is afebrile at presentation","Any patient already taking antibiotics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cellulitis is usually a clinical diagnosis, and no workup is needed when there is a limited area of involvement, minimal pain, no systemic signs, and no risk factor for serious illness such as extremes of age or immunocompromise. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 44-year-old fair-skinned woman newly diagnosed with rosacea asks which trigger is most important to control. What is the most accurate answer?","choices":["Alcohol, described as the most universal trigger","Dairy products, which drive Demodex overgrowth","Topical corticosteroid use, which is the primary cause","Sun exposure, described as the most universal trigger","Cold weather, which is the dominant environmental trigger"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sun exposure is named as the most universal trigger of rosacea, alongside heat, alcohol, spicy foods, and stress. Chronic ultraviolet exposure is also a listed risk factor. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Four women in their twenties present on the same clinic list with acne of differing severity and history. For which patient is a combined oral contraceptive most appropriate as acne therapy?","choices":["A man with moderate papulopustular acne","A woman with mild comedonal acne who has not yet used a topical retinoid or antibiotic of any kind","A woman planning pregnancy in the next three months","A woman with hyperandrogenism whose acne has not responded to topical retinoids and antibiotics","Any adolescent as first-line therapy regardless of sex or severity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oral contraceptives are considered for patients with hyperandrogenism and when acne is unresponsive to both topical retinoids and topical or oral antibiotics. Oestrogen decreases sebum production and reduces ovarian androgen production. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 29-year-old fair-skinned woman asks how to use sunscreen correctly before a beach holiday. What advice matches the guidance given?","choices":["Apply sunscreen only to patients with Fitzpatrick skin types I to III","Use a broad-spectrum product with sun protection factor of at least 30 daily, applying it 15 minutes before exposure and reapplying every 2 hours","Use a broad-spectrum product with sun protection factor of at least 30 daily, applying it once at the start of the day and not reapplying it again","Use a product with sun protection factor 15 and reapply only after swimming","Rely on physical barriers alone, since sunscreen provides no measurable benefit"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Broad-spectrum sun protection factor 30 or above is advised for daily use and 50 or above for prolonged outdoor exposure, applied 15 minutes before sun exposure and reapplied every 2 hours. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man presents with a single intensely itchy patch on the posterior neck that has been present for over a year. He describes rubbing it frequently, particularly when stressed or trying to fall asleep. Examination shows a well-demarcated thickened plaque with exaggerated skin markings, excoriation, scale, and altered pigmentation. What is the central principle of management?","choices":["Reassurance alone, since the condition resolves spontaneously once the patient stops scratching","Breaking the itch-scratch cycle through education, behavioural substitution, nail care, and safe occlusion","Surgical excision of the thickened plaque","A prolonged course of a potent topical corticosteroid continued indefinitely","An oral antifungal, since chronic thickened plaques are usually dermatophytic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lichen simplex chronicus results from repeated rubbing and scratching, so management centres on breaking the itch-scratch cycle with education, treatment of the trigger, emollients, behavioural substitution, nail care, and safe physical occlusion, alongside a limited course of an appropriate potent topical corticosteroid. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 183"},{"q":"A 78-year-old fair-skinned farmer has a small red, conical, hard nodule on the pinna that has ulcerated and grown over two months. What is the most likely diagnosis and its relationship to premalignant disease?","choices":["Basal cell carcinoma, the most common form of cancer overall, arising from intense intermittent sun exposure in fair-skinned people","Actinic keratosis, a premalignant lesion","Amelanotic melanoma","Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, the second most common skin cancer, which may arise from an actinic keratosis","Keratoacanthoma, which regresses spontaneously"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma is the second most common form of skin cancer, usually following prolonged cumulative sun exposure, and may arise from an actinic keratosis. The classic presentation is a small red conical hard nodule that may ulcerate, or a non-healing ulcer, warty nodule, or irregular pink plaque with haemorrhagic crust. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has several soft whitish-yellow papules 3 to 5 mm on the forehead, each with a central dell. He is worried about skin cancer. What test can distinguish this from the main differential?","choices":["Dermoscopy, which can distinguish sebaceous hyperplasia from basal cell carcinoma","A Wood's lamp examination","Serum lipid measurement","A potassium hydroxide preparation","No test is available to distinguish them, so all such lesions must be excised"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Basal cell carcinoma is the principal differential for sebaceous hyperplasia, and dermoscopy can distinguish between them. Biopsy is performed if malignancy concern persists. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has tinea corporis, and the clinician wants an antifungal that works by destroying the fungal cell membrane rather than by blocking ergosterol synthesis. Which agent belongs to that class, and how is the class recognised by name?","choices":["Terbinafine, an imidazole, recognisable by the ending -azole","Nystatin, a polyene that treats both Candida and dermatophytes","Ketoconazole, an imidazole, recognisable by the ending -fine","Griseofulvin, an allylamine favoured for Trichophyton infection","Terbinafine, an allylamine, recognisable by the ending -fine"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The allylamine class works by destroying the cell membranes of fungi, preventing growth and ultimately killing them, and its members end in -fine: terbinafine and naftifine. Imidazoles instead block synthesis of ergosterol, a vital component of the fungal cell membrane. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman and her 62-year-old husband, who takes an immunosuppressant, both ask about shingles vaccination. Who should receive the recombinant zoster vaccine, and at what interval?","choices":["Two doses for children as part of the routine childhood schedule","A single dose for immunocompetent adults aged 50 and over","Two doses for immunocompetent adults aged 50 and over, and two doses for adults aged 19 and over who are or will be immunodeficient or immunosuppressed, at a standard interval of 2 to 6 months","Two doses for immunocompetent adults aged 65 and over only","Two doses only for adults who have already had an episode of herpes zoster, and a single dose for immunocompetent adults aged 50 and over, at an interval of 2 to 6 months regardless of immune status"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two doses of recombinant zoster vaccine are given to immunocompetent adults aged 50 years and over, and two doses to adults aged 19 and over who are or will be immunodeficient or immunosuppressed, with a standard interval of 2 to 6 months. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with deeply pigmented skin asks whether she needs sun protection at all. What does the Fitzpatrick skin type classification predict, and how should it guide counselling?","choices":["It predicts the likelihood of developing rosacea and guides trigger avoidance","It predicts baseline ultraviolet sensitivity and guides photoprotection counselling, though all types remain susceptible to cumulative damage","It grades the severity of a sunburn once it has occurred","It determines the required sun protection factor, with no other measures needed","It predicts baseline ultraviolet sensitivity and guides photoprotection counselling, and types V and VI require no counselling at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fitzpatrick skin type predicts baseline ultraviolet sensitivity and guides photoprotection counselling. All types are susceptible to cumulative ultraviolet damage, photoaging, and skin cancer, though absolute risk varies. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 122"}]};
